  "explanation": {
    "easy": {
      "heading": "A Smooth Start to the Day",
      "body": "Starting the day with the easy puzzle was a real treat. I immediately looked for the easiest anchors, which were the zero sum targets at cells 0,0 and 0,3. Since these spots have to be empty or zero, I knew right away where to place the 0,0 domino. I paired the first zero with 1,0 and the second with 1,3. It felt like laying down the first few pieces of a corner in a puzzle.\n\nFrom there, the rest of the board started to speak to me. The region at 1,0 and 1,1 needed a sum of 3. Since I already had a 1 at cell 1,0 from my 1,0 domino, I just needed a 2 at cell 1,1. This led me to use the 4,2 domino across cells 1,2 and 1,1. Once those were in place, the equals region at the bottom was a breeze to finish up using the 5,1 and 5,0 dominoes. It is all about following the trail of numbers!"
    },
    "medium": {
      "heading": "Thinking Outside the Box",
      "body": "The medium puzzle today really made me slow down and think about the bigger numbers. I saw those sum targets of 11 right away at cells 2,0 and 3,0, as well as the 4,2 and 4,3 area. Since the highest pip on any domino is a six, a sum of 11 can only be made with a 5 and a 6. I grabbed my 6,5 domino and placed it vertically on the left side, which felt like a huge win.\n\nThings got a little trickier in the middle with the less than and greater than signs. Cell 0,3 had to be greater than 3, so I used the 3 end of the 0,3 domino there, paired with cell 1,3. For the unequal region at 1,2 and 1,3, I just had to make sure the values did not match. By the time I got to the 1,1 cell, which had to be less than 3, I used the 1,1 domino to satisfy both the inequality and the equal sign region below it. It was like a little logic dance!"
    },
    "hard": {
      "heading": "The Ultimate Domino Challenge",
      "body": "Wow, sixteen dominoes is no joke! My breakthrough moment came when I spotted the sum target of 18 in the middle of the board for cells 3,1, 3,2, and 4,1. To get 18 from only three cells, every single one of them has to be a 6. That meant I had to find three different dominoes that each had a 6 to place in those specific spots. I used the 3,6 and 6,6 dominoes along with the 6,2 piece to make it work.\n\nAfter clearing that big hurdle, I focused on the empty regions at 5,3, 5,4, and 5,5. Since these are restricted, they actually help narrow down where your dominoes can go. I spent a bit of time stuck on the sum of 6 at the very top, but once I realized I could use the 5,5 domino for cells 5,5 and 4,5, the rest of the bottom half fell into place. Finishing this one felt like a real accomplishment after a few dead ends on the left side!"
    },
    "tips": "Always look for the largest and smallest sum targets first because they have the fewest possible combinations. If you see a sum of 12, you know it must be two 6s, and a sum of 0 must be two 0s. Also, use the empty cells as boundaries to help you visualize where a domino can or cannot fit.",
    "learned": "Today I learned how powerful the equals constraints are when they are adjacent to large sum targets. In the hard puzzle, seeing that 18 target really forced my hand with the 6s, which then made the neighboring less than 2 and sum 10 regions much easier to solve. It is all about finding that one big clue that causes a chain reaction across the board.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do you play NYT Pips?",
        "answer": "In Pips, you place dominoes on a grid to satisfy various regional constraints like sums, equalities, or inequalities. Each domino can only be used once, and every cell must be filled."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does the target sum mean in Pips?",
        "answer": "The target sum is the total number of pips that must be present within a specific outlined region. For example, if a region of two cells has a target of 5, the two pips in those cells must add up to exactly 5."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can dominoes be placed diagonally in the puzzle?",
        "answer": "No, dominoes must always be placed either horizontally or vertically across two adjacent cells. Diagonal placement is not allowed."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is an empty region in Pips?",
        "answer": "An empty region typically means there are no specific mathematical constraints like a sum or an inequality for those cells, though they still must be filled by dominoes following the standard rules."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are all dominoes in the list used in every puzzle?",
        "answer": "Yes, every domino provided in the list for a specific difficulty must be used exactly once to complete the grid and satisfy all the logic requirements."
      }
    ]
